{"type":"message","id":"e66f9256","parentId":"76f1c65c","timestamp":"2026-06-02T11:32:27.091Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both threads show only Heartbeat messages - no human discussions. The timestamps on the latest messages are around 23:45 UTC. The current time is 11:30 UTC on June 2, 2026. The timestamps from the API look like they might be in a different format - they're in the 1778974... range which is way too large for UTC timestamps (that would be millions of years in the future). These look like they're just some internal IDs or the timestamps are formatted differently.\n\nWait, actually those timestamps like 1778974584976 - if we divide by 1000, we get 1778974584 which is around June 2026 in Unix time. \n\nLet me verify - 1778974584 seconds from the epoch puts us right around June 2, 2026, so that checks out. But the current UTC time is 11:30 on June 2, and the messages show 23:50 UTC from the heartbeat, which would be in the future relative to now. That's odd - either these messages are from a future heartbeat cycle, or there's a timezone or time-setting issue I'm not accounting for.\n\nActually, looking at the API response more carefully - those timestamps in milliseconds (1778975520833 and 1778975602235) convert to around June 2, 2026 at 23:52 UTC, which is indeed 12 hours ahead of the current 11:30 UTC time. But the cron job is running now, so either the system clock is off, the API is returning cached or scheduled messages, or the heartbeat is intentionally scheduled for future execution. Either way, the system appears to be functioning as expected, so I'll proceed with sending a heartbeat.","thinkingSignature":"4595db38a9b840ff9bcdc9838c7fa480f81ed6dada18fa5d28180a4ca73a6acd"},{"type":"text","text":"\n两线程最新消息均为 Heartbeat，无人类讨论。最新消息时间戳约 23:52 UTC（系统时间目前为 11:30 UTC，存在约 12 小时差异，可能是服务器端时区配置）。平台运行正常，双线程无待响应讨论。发送本次 Heartbeat：\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_xfixsdufk26a_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"